@page "/learn/web-worker-template"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>.NET in a Web Worker · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">.NET in a Web Worker</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">Your WebAssembly page totals a big CSV export. How do you keep it responsive while it works?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · WebAssembly · Interactive WebAssembly · about 6 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> a project template, <code>blazorwebworker</code>, that writes the plumbing for
        running .NET in a Web Worker into your project, as source you own. No framework API changed. .NET 10 could
        already do it, if you wrote that plumbing yourself.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a <i>Revenue by product</i> report. It downloads last year's order lines as a CSV export, then
        totals them in the browser, on WebAssembly, and lists the top five products.
    </p>
    <p>
        A clock on the page ticks ten times a second, on the UI thread. Each run adds a line: how long it took, and
        how often the clock ticked out of the times it should have. Fig. 1 has no worker: the method runs on the UI
        thread, the way a .NET 10 app would run it. It's running for real, on this page's .NET 11 runtime; the
        behaviour is the same on both.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The report on the UI thread" Url="demo/web-worker/order-lines/net10"
                Label="No worker: on the UI thread" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            When it says <b>Ready</b>, press <b>Total on the UI thread</b>. The clock freezes until the top five
            appear, and the run line says it ticked <b>0</b> times. Fig. 2's clock stops too: every demo on this page
            shares the tab's one UI thread. Then press <b>Total, pausing every 1,000 lines</b>, a .NET 10 workaround.
            The clock keeps ticking, but the run takes longer.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>One thread does everything</h2>
    <p>Blazor WebAssembly runs your C# on the page's UI thread, in .NET 11 too. That thread has a queue of jobs:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The browser's UI thread, while the report runs</span>
        <pre class="snip">UI thread <span class="dim">(one per tab)</span>
├─ TotalOrderLines(csv)   <span class="err">runs to the end, seconds</span>
├─ clock tick             <span class="dim">waits</span>
├─ draw the page          <span class="dim">waits</span>
└─ your click             <span class="dim">waits</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        The loop never yields, so nothing else gets a turn until it finishes. The work isn't slow because it's on the
        UI thread. It's just in the way.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Run the same method in a Web Worker</h2>
    <p>
        A Web Worker is a second thread the browser gives you. Make a worker library from the template, add a
        project reference to it from the WebAssembly client, and call your method by name:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="hl">dotnet new blazorwebworker -n NorthwindOps.Worker</span>

<span class="dim">// NorthwindOps.Worker/WorkerMethods.cs</span>
<span class="hl">[JSExport]</span>
public static string TotalOrderLines(byte[] csv) { ... }

<span class="dim">&lt;!-- NorthwindOps.Client.csproj --&gt;</span>
<span class="hl">&lt;ProjectReference Include="../NorthwindOps.Worker/NorthwindOps.Worker.csproj" /&gt;</span>

<span class="dim">// The client component</span>
worker = await <span class="hl">WebWorkerClient.CreateAsync(JS)</span>;
var json = await worker.<span class="hl">InvokeAsync&lt;string&gt;</span>(
    "NorthwindOps.Worker.WorkerMethods.TotalOrderLines", [csv]);</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        <code>WebWorkerClient</code> is a class the template wrote into the library. It starts a second .NET
        runtime in a worker, and copies the bytes across. The method runs there, and the UI thread stays free to
        tick and draw. The template's two scripts sit in the library's <code>wwwroot</code>; the reference serves
        them at <code>_content/NorthwindOps.Worker/</code>, and <code>WebWorkerClient</code> loads them itself, so
        <code>App.razor</code> doesn't change. Here's the same report with the worker.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same report, in a Web Worker" Url="demo/web-worker/order-lines"
                Label=".NET 11 template: in a Web Worker">
        <Caption>
            When it says <b>Ready</b>, the worker has started. Press <b>Total in a Web Worker</b>. The same top five
            arrive, every tick is counted, and the run takes about as long as the first one in Fig. 1: the worker isn't
            faster, the work has just moved. Optionally, press <b>Call a misspelled method</b>: it asks for
            <code>TotalOrderLine</code>, without the <i>s</i>, and the status shows "Method not found". The name is
            only checked at run time, and the worker's error comes back to the call as an exception.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Both reports call the same method. Here are the three files:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Client/WebWorker/OrderLineReportNet10.razor" Feature="web-worker-template">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 1's component. It imports <code>NorthwindOps.Worker</code> only to reuse the same
            <code>TotalOrderLines</code> method, and calls it as plain C# on the UI thread; no worker is started.
            The second method is the pausing workaround.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Client/WebWorker/OrderLineReport.razor" Feature="web-worker-template">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 2's component. It starts the worker once, after its first render, calls the method by name, and
            disposes of the worker when the component goes. Where its comments say "New in .NET 11", they mean new
            with the template: <code>WebWorkerClient</code>, <code>InvokeAsync</code> and <code>DisposeAsync</code> are
            the template's generated source, not framework types.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="5" Path="Worker/WorkerMethods.cs" Feature="web-worker-template">
        <Caption>
            The method both figures run: a static <code>[JSExport]</code> method, bytes in and a JSON string out.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Pause, offload, or write the worker yourself</h2>
    <p>
        Nothing here is broken in .NET 10. The repro's UI-thread run took 7.9 s, the clock ticked 0 of about 79
        times, and the browser drew no frame for 7.9 s. Apps had three ways out:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Three .NET 10 workarounds, and what they cost</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">if (++lines % 1000 == 0)
    <span class="hl">await Task.Delay(1);</span></pre>
                <span class="why">Fig. 1's second button; the repro kept every tick (92 of 92), but took 9.2 s instead of 7.9 s</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ about 17% slower, and every heavy loop needs its own pauses</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">await Http.PostAsync(
    "api/reports/order-lines", content);</pre>
                <span class="why">post the export, and let the server total it</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ another API to build and secure, and no report when the server can't be reached</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">import { dotnet } from '../_framework/dotnet.js';
const { getAssemblyExports, getConfig } =
    await dotnet.create();
<span class="dim">// ...match replies to requests yourself</span></pre>
                <span class="why">one of five files Microsoft's guide had you write</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ request ids, errors, timeouts and start-up failures all up to you</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        The third is the real fix, and it already worked in .NET 10. It was just a lot of code to write, test and keep working.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>The worker, written for you</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11, <code>dotnet new blazorwebworker</code> writes that code into your project: <code>WorkerMethods.cs</code>
        for your methods, <code>WebWorkerClient.cs</code> (69 lines of C#), and two scripts (157 lines). The scripts
        start the worker and its runtime, match each reply to its request, turn the worker's errors into exceptions,
        and time calls out after 60 seconds. It's source you own, not a framework API, so you can read and change all
        of it. That's what Fig. 2 runs.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>Not faster, just elsewhere.</b> The worker runs the same code at the same speed. Copying the data across even adds a little.</li>
        <li><b>WebAssembly only.</b> The worker boots the WebAssembly app's runtime, so reference the library from the client project. It does nothing for Interactive Server.</li>
        <li><b>Found by name, at run time.</b> A typo or a rename only fails when called, as Fig. 2 shows. Only simple values cross over: strings, numbers and byte arrays, so send JSON for the rest.</li>
        <li><b>A timeout stops the wait, not the work.</b> Both <code>CreateAsync</code> (start-up) and <code>InvokeAsync</code> (each call) take a <code>timeoutMs</code> argument, 60,000 by default; pass a bigger one for longer jobs. When it fires, the worker finishes the method anyway, and the next call waits behind it. Each worker is a whole second .NET runtime, so start one, keep it, and dispose of it when the component goes.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
